  "bibliography": {
    "abstract": "Clarifying the complex mechanism through which the functional polycentric structure (FPS) of urban agglomerations affects carbon emission performance (CEPE) is crucial for formulating low-carbon and sustainable spatial planning strategies. Current research often adopts a single perspective, presenting challenges in theoretical integration and methodological applicability. This study innovatively integrated configuration theory, space of flows theory, and total-factor productivity theory to refine intercity factor flows into capital, population, technology, and information flows, thereby systematically constructing a theoretical framework that elucidates how the FPS of urban agglomerations influences CEPE. On this basis, this study drew on empirical data from 12 representative urban agglomerations in China to propose differentiated spatial planning pathways for enhancing CEPE. The CEPE of urban agglomerations was affected by the combined effects of multi-dimensional FPS rather than the independent effects widely explored in existing studies. Three configurational pathways of FPS led to high CEPE: a population flow polycentricity driven pathway suitable for nurturing urban agglomerations, an information-capital synergistic driven pathway suitable for developing urban agglomerations, and a capital-dominant, technology-assisted driven pathway suitable for mature urban agglomerations. These representative and diverse pathways can provide scientific insights for optimizing the spatial structure of urban agglomerations and achieving multiple sustainable development goals, including low-carbon transition, regional synergy, and efficiency improvement.",
