  "summary": {
    "en": {
      "caveats": "The findings are based on a configuration analysis of 12 specific urban agglomerations in China, which may limit their direct applicability to different national or institutional planning contexts.",
      "what": "This study analyzed how functional polycentric structures affect carbon emission performance across 12 representative urban agglomerations in China. By integrating configuration, space of flows, and total-factor productivity theories, the research mapped intercity factor flows across capital, population, technology, and information networks to identify three distinct spatial pathways that lead to high carbon efficiency.",
      "why": "It demonstrates that carbon emission performance is driven by the combined, synergistic effects of multi-dimensional functional networks rather than isolated spatial structures, offering tailored spatial planning strategies for urban agglomerations at different stages of maturity."
    }
